-- CK-28: the same head matching (static-dispatch-spike.md §7.1, A.64;
-- checker-v2.md §9.3 step 4) for a head with TWO requirements:
-- `H.eq : Holder ( a, b ), … where a.eq …, b.eq …` has two parts while
-- `Holder` has one type argument, so an index-by-index mapping cannot even
-- be built. The second element is a `Nocase.Ci`, whose own `eq` ignores
-- case, so `W`'s comparison of `Ci "a"` with `Ci "A"` is `True` only if
-- `H.eq` really receives `Ci.eq` for `b` — a structural `W` would say
-- `False`. Expected `True`, `True`, `False`.
--
-- Oracle twin, run on 7427828: the direct comparison, the first line of
-- this program, compiles alone and prints `True`; the `W` lines are the same
-- comparison one wrapper down. As written, 7427828 refuses `W` with NOT
-- IMPLEMENTED YET, whose message ("a `Target.ext` carries no range of its
-- own") has been false since A.64.
pub type Holder a
    = Holder a


pub eq : Holder ( a, b ), Holder ( a, b ) -> Bool
    where a.eq : a, a -> Bool, b.eq : b, b -> Bool
eq left right =
    case left of
        Holder ( la, lb ) ->
            case right of
                Holder ( ra, rb ) ->
                    la == ra && lb == rb
